 "reading_text": "The user is asking whether an omphalocele is present in the image. Let me look carefully at the clinical photograph.\n\nWhat I see: A newborn infant, supine, with a gloved hand holding up a large, reddish, lobulated mass protruding from the abdominal wall. There's also what appears to be a wrapped leg and an arm, suggesting this is a complex congenital anomaly. The mass is being held above the abdomen.\n\nKey features to distinguish omphalocele from gastroschisis from a large hernia:\n- Omphalocele: a midline abdominal wall defect at the umbilicus, with herniated contents covered by a membranous sac (peritoneum/amnion), typically with a single stalk connecting to the umbilicus, and the umbilical cord inserts into the apex of the sac. The sac is usually intact and translucent, not reddish and thick.\n- Gastroschisis: paraumbilical defect (usually right), bowel eviscerated WITHOUT a covering sac, often with bowel that is thickened, reddish, and edematous due to amniotic fluid exposure.\n- The mass here looks lobulated, reddish-brown, and is being held up. The base connects to the lower abdomen. There's also a phallus visible (the penis is prominent).\n\n",
